Collections (articles, research) and the landing page are being implemented from these decisions.

Reference to review: [Scalar](https://www.shadcnblocks.com/template/scalar) is an excellent article layout.

Collection support is declared in `vitepress.site.config.json`, so each documentation site can opt into collections such as articles or research:

```json
{
  "collections": [
    { "id": "collection-1-id", "path": "path-1/" },
    { "id": "collection-2-id", "path": "path-2/" }
  ]
}
```

Collection folders own their metadata files, such as `category.json`, alongside their content. Keep the site generator agnostic: a site opts into collections through its config, while collection metadata and content stay in the configured folders.

## Visual and implementation references

- [Vite landing hero source](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/main/docs/.vitepress/theme/landing/Hero.vue): two-column VitePress/Vue hero, install-command tabs, original Rive animation, clear paired actions. Reuse the original source/assets only if selected; do not recreate the animation or its styling.
- [Vite aside sponsors source](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/main/docs/.vitepress/theme/components/AsideSponsors.vue): ViteConf callout and sponsor block, populated through a composable and `VPDocAsideSponsors`. Reference for a compact sidebar promo block.
- [Vue.js site](https://vuejs.org/) and [Vue docs source](https://github.com/vuejs/docs): inspect the official VitePress theme and reusable layout patterns as further references.
- [Scalar](https://www.shadcnblocks.com/template/scalar): preferred article/research visual reference. It is a paid Next.js/Astro template; its Astro edition uses Astro content collections and Markdown/MDX. Visual reference only until licensing/source and exact porting scope are approved.
- [Mainline](https://www.shadcnblocks.com/template/mainline): selected home source. Its public repository is free to download and uses an MIT license with a Commons Clause restriction. Port relevant blocks to Vue while retaining the source layout and utility styling; retain attribution and license in `.components/home/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt`.

## Implemented from these notes

- The home page is a close Vue port of the complete Mainline page: Hero, Logos, Features, Resource Allocation, Testimonials, Pricing, FAQ, and Footer. It uses Mainline's original sample text and copied image assets as placeholders, with block data passed as explicit Vue props directly from `docs/guides/index.en.md`; no `$frontmatter` object is used. The VitePress site header is retained. Preserve Mainline attribution and license terms in `.components/home/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt`.
- The generator consumes `collections` entries `{ "id": "articles", "path": "learn/" }`. IDs define the public collection routes; source folders hold content and `category.json`. Metadata supports localized `title`, `description`, and category entries (`id`, `label`). Collection pages list Markdown records from the configured folder and fall back to the default language. The current site config exposes `/articles/` from `learn/` and `/research/` from `internals/`.
- Scalar remains the preferred article visual reference. Its template is paid and its source has not been acquired, so its components and styling are not copied or reconstructed. Current collection pages prove the data flow; a Scalar-based Vue port still requires licensed source material.
- Vite's Hero animation and ViteConf sponsor callout remain references only because their assets and sponsor details belong to Vite. Do not copy those blocks without authentic site-specific assets or event data.
